<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/docs/Code.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Code code={`abide bundle                    # ./ledger.app
abide bundle --out ~/Applications/Ledger.app`} />

    <p>The icon is <code>src/public/icon.png</code>, a square image at 1024 pixels, drawn at every
    size the Dock and Finder ask for. An <code>icon.icns</code> beside it is used as it is
    instead. Any public file the app answers at <code>/icon.png</code> will do. The app is named
    by its package, as the executable is.</p>

    <p>Read on: <a href={url('/ship/ship-a-single-binary')}>Compile</a></p>

    <Section title="The first launch asks where the app is">
        <p>The window opens on the app's icon, its name and two buttons. <strong>Start</strong> runs
        the app in this process, served on loopback for the window alone. <strong>Connect…</strong>
        opens one already running at a URL, the same app deployed somewhere a team shares. Its color
        is your icon's: the launcher takes the most saturated hue in it, so a teal mark on a dark
        square gives teal buttons.</p>

        <p>A button with <code>…</code> asks something first, in a dialog. Start asks for each field
        your <code>onConfig</code> schema declares, and an app with nothing to configure has a plain
        <strong>Start</strong> that starts at once. Its title
        is the label, its <code>description</code> is the hint, and its type picks the input: a
        checkbox for a boolean, a number for a number, a menu for an <code>enum</code>. A schema
        with no JSON Schema to read gets one field per default <code>onConfig</code> returns
        instead. What you type is the environment the app boots under, so it is coerced and checked
        exactly as a deployment's variables are, and a value the schema refuses comes back under its
        field.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/app/configure-the-app')}>Config</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The next launch does what the last one did">
        <p>The launch remembers the choice in <code>APP_DATA_DIR</code>. If you started the app with
        these values, the next launch starts it with them without asking. If you connected, it
        connects to the same URL. The local app gets the same port each time where that port is
        free, so cookies and storage survive a restart.</p>

        <p><strong>Disconnect</strong> in the File menu stops the app served here and goes back to
        the forms, and the next launch opens there too. <strong>Connect…</strong> opens the forms
        without stopping anything until you choose.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`launcher` sets the window and the forms">
        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`<script module>
import type { LauncherOptions } from '@abide/abide/server'

export const launcher: LauncherOptions = {
    width: 960,
    height: 640,
    connect: false,
}
</script>`} />

        <p><code>src/launcher.abide</code> sits beside your root layout, and the
        <code>launcher</code> its <code>&lt;script module&gt;</code> exports is read when the app
        launches.
        A file of that block alone keeps the built-in page. <code>width</code> and
        <code>height</code> are the first window's size, and <code>minWidth</code> and
        <code>minHeight</code> its floor. After the first launch, the window opens where it was last
        left.</p>

        <p><code>start</code> and <code>connect</code> turn a button off. An app that only ever talks
        to its server turns <code>start</code> off. An app that only runs locally turns
        <code>connect</code> off, and Connect… leaves the File menu with it. Turning both off is a
        type error, because the launcher would have nothing to offer, and an app that slips one
        past the checker refuses to launch.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`launcher.abide` is the launch page, in your own markup">
        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`<script>
import { props } from '@abide/abide'
import type { Launch } from '@abide/abide/server'

const { name, start, fields, failure } = props<Launch>()
</script>

<h1>Welcome to {name}</h1>
{#if failure}<p>{failure.message}</p>{/if}
{#if start}
    <form method="post" action={start}>
        {#for field of fields by field.name}
            <label>
                {field.label}
                <input name={field.name} value={field.value}>
            </label>
        {/for}
        <button>Open {name}</button>
    </form>
{/if}`} />

        <p>Markup in <code>src/launcher.abide</code> replaces the built-in page, and receives the
        same <code>Launch</code> the built-in page is drawn from. <code>start</code> and
        <code>connect</code> are the addresses the two forms post to, and each is
        <code>undefined</code> where <code>launcher</code> turned that form off.
        <code>fields</code> is one <code>ConfigField</code> per input the start form asks for,
        prefilled. <code>failure</code> is what the last attempt was refused with, and
        <code>icon</code> and <code>accent</code> are the icon and the color the built-in page
        uses.</p>

        <p>It is rendered by <code>render</code> before the app it belongs to is running, so it is a
        document rather than a live page: its forms post and its <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> applies,
        but an <code>onclick</code> does nothing. Written inside <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>, it
        writes its own document. A launch page that fails to render reaches
        <code>onError</code>, and the built-in page shows in its place.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/render-a-document-yourself')}>Manual rendering</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A page adds to the menu bar by exporting `menu`">
        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`<script module>
import { menuItem } from '@abide/abide'

export const exportInvoices = menuItem({ label: 'Export…', key: 'cmd+e' })
export const menu = [
    { label: 'File', items: ['separator', exportInvoices] },
    {
        label: 'Invoices',
        items: [{ label: 'Overdue', href: '/invoices/overdue' }],
    },
]
</script>

<script>
import { watch } from '@abide/abide'

watch(() => exportInvoices.on(() => download()))
</script>`} />

        <p>The menu bar is the route's. Every desktop app has the app's own menu, File, Edit, View
        and Window, and each layout around the page and then the page itself adds what its
        <code>&lt;script module&gt;</code> exports as <code>menu</code>. A layout's menus are there
        on every page under it, and a page's are there only while it is showing. Navigate away and
        they go.</p>

        <p>A menu with the same <code>label</code> as one already there adds its items to the end of
        it, so Export… lands in File. Any other menu stands before Window, where macOS puts an app's
        own.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>An item</th><th>When picked</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>made by <code>menuItem</code></td><td>runs every function its <code>on</code> filed, in the page that exported it</td></tr>
                <tr><td>with an <code>href</code></td><td>navigates there, as a link in the page would</td></tr>
                <tr><td>with a <code>role</code></td><td>does what the platform does: <code>copy</code> copies, <code>quit</code> stops the app and quits</td></tr>
                <tr><td>with <code>items</code></td><td>is a menu of its own</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>'separator'</code> is a line between two items, and <code>key</code> is the
        shortcut, modifiers first.</p>

        <p><code>on</code> hands back what stops it, which is what <code>watch</code> takes, so the
        answer goes when the page does. The page answers whether the app is served here or connected
        to: a remote app's pages bring their own menus. Outside a desktop app nothing picks an item,
        and the same page runs in a browser unchanged.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/give-pages-the-same-chrome')}>Layouts</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
